From 6e05d53cf8de866f021e845dd0ebf9ea43fa10ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 18:18:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] MV-HEVC: second-view sets found in a
 Matroska track's packets, and Apple's stereo metadata read

What the demuxer decides of a track of two views moves into
CDVDDemuxFFmpeg::ChooseTwoViews(), unchanged for MVC and for MV-HEVC with
the parameter sets of its second view in its private data, so that it can
be decided again while the track is read. Two things are added for
MV-HEVC:

- A Matroska track made by FFmpeg or mkvmerge keeps the parameter sets of
  the base layer only in its CodecPrivate (measured with FFmpeg 9.1: its
  Matroska muxer writes the record again without those of other layers),
  and stayed 2D. Where the stream it was made of carried the second
  layer's sets in its samples (an elementary stream, an MP4 file of
  'hev1'), they are in its packets still. Such a track, declared as two
  views by its video parameter set and otherwise eligible, is looked
  through (KODI::MULTIVIEW::LookInPacket(), in the order of its NAL units):
  where the sequence and picture parameter sets of the second layer come
  before its first slice, they are appended to the private data as FFmpeg
  appends those of 'lhvC' (HvccWithSets()), the track is decided again,
  and the player opens its codec again (changes) before that packet is
  decoded, so a 3D display switches to 3D once, before the first picture
  is shown. Where the first slice of the second layer comes without them,
  or a packet does not hold together, or 120 packets pass, the track stays
  the 2D stream it was: its second view would not be decoded there, and a
  switch to 3D later is what is avoided. kodi.log says which.
- Apple's spatial video describes how it was made in 'vexu' ('eyes':
  'hero', 'cams'/'blin', 'cmfy'/'dadj'; 'hfov'), which FFmpeg reads into
  AVStereo3D. Its disparity adjustment goes to the decoder
  (CDemuxStreamVideo::multiviewDisparity, CDVDStreamInfo) and to the
  packer, which moves each eye by half of it: the cameras of a spatial
  video look in parallel, which puts everything far away at the plane of
  the screen and the rest in front of it, and the adjustment is where its
  maker puts that plane instead, as the Vision Pro shows it. The hero eye,
  the distance between the cameras and their field of view are written to
  kodi.log: on a flat 3D display they change nothing that can be shown
  right.

Checked on the host (tools/regress/mvhevc): Matroska files made of MP4
files of the conformance streams A and E, of 'hev1': the sets found in the
first packet, the private data with them is two views, and the file
decoded with it gives each view as the reference decoder; of 'hvc1': not
there, 2D. The rules of the search on made-up packets (sets after the
first slice, without an SPS, a cut packet, 120 packets without the layer,
lengths of 2 and 4 bytes); its mutants caught. The demuxer itself, and
reading 'vexu' from a real spatial video, are not tried here.
